The output does not meet the work item. The task was to remove duplicate respondents and nothing else. The raw file has 17,157 rows and 155 duplicate respondents, so the clean file should have about 17,002 rows (17,157 − 155) if each duplicate is a single extra row. Instead it has 2,873 rows, about 14,284 fewer than that. The respondent_id check (2,873 unique of 2,873) only confirms the output has no duplicates, not that the right rows were kept. Dropping 155 duplicates cannot account for a loss of more than 14,000 rows, so the step removed respondents that were not duplicates, which the work item prohibits.

The weight total matching the target population (4,781,404) is consistent with recalibration, but it was computed on the wrong set of respondents, so the weights are not valid for the intended population. The file would need to be regenerated with only the duplicate rows removed, then reweighted and checked for 17,002 rows.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Output has 2,873 rows instead of about 17,002 (raw 17,157 minus 155 duplicates), so non-duplicate respondents were dropped, violating the 'nothing else may be dropped' requirement and invalidating the recalibrated weights."}

The work item did not succeed. The job log shows an inner merge with `respondents_meta.csv` that took the data from 17,002 rows to 2,873. That drops 14,129 rows beyond the 155 duplicates, which the task explicitly forbids ("nothing else may be dropped"). The validation report's "duplicates removed 155" label is misleading, since 17,157 − 155 = 17,002, not 2,873.

The weights do sum to the 4,781,404 target and the respondent IDs are unique, but those checks pass on the truncated dataset. The agent's "All checks green" report did not mention the merge-induced loss, and the output file of 2,873 rows is not a valid clean version of D3.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "An inner merge with respondents_meta.csv silently dropped about 14,129 rows beyond the 155 duplicates (17,002 to 2,873), violating the 'nothing else may be dropped' requirement."}

The output does not look right. The runbook lists 749 known duplicate respondents in D9. Removing those from the 37,073 raw rows should leave 36,324 rows. The file has 36,863, so 210 rows were removed, not 749. That is 539 fewer than expected, and the output's unique respondent_id count (36,863 of 36,863) shows that the removal was done by ID. Either the deduplication missed re-interviews, or the rule that "nothing else may be dropped" was broken in some other way. Only 210 rows left the dataset, so the count tells us the result is wrong, but not which of those two causes applies.

The weights do hit the target. The sum is exactly 3,821,233, and the range and mean are plausible. The income code -9 (refused) is correctly kept, per the runbook. The null counts look ordinary and don't bear on the task. Those points don't offset the row-count mismatch, which is the core requirement of the work item.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.82, "reason": "Only 210 rows were removed instead of the 749 known duplicate respondents (expected 36,324 rows, got 36,863), so deduplication is incomplete or other rows were dropped, even though the weights sum correctly to the target."}

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The job removed exactly the 237 duplicate respondents (26,325 to 26,088 rows, no other rows dropped), weights sum to the 8,051,887 target, the weighted female share matches census, and the schema and codebook checks pass."}

The output file matches the stated intent on the main points. I'm not fully certain because the log says weights were "rescaled" to the total, which is not the same as demographic recalibration. The one demographic check (female share) does pass, which supports the weights being usable. The 212 "non-numeric" income codes are unexplained in the log, but the runbook says the -9 refused code is kept unchanged, so this does not look like data loss.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "The job removed exactly the 237 duplicate respondents (26,325 to 26,088 rows, no other rows dropped), weights sum to the 8,051,887 target, the weighted female share matches census, and the schema and codebook checks pass."}

The invoice was not sent to the intended billing contact. The work item specified l.huber@lindner-logistik.at, but the CRM activity records the send to l.kraus@lindner-logistik.at. The agent's "All checks green" report doesn't account for this.

The rest checks out: the amount (€ 8,358.55), the due date (2026-10-23 plus 14 days is 2026-11-06), the PDF attachment, and the archive and dunning entries all match. But the core goal was delivery to the billing contact, and the recipient is wrong. Unless there is a documented reason that K-67's billing contact is l.kraus (the runbook doesn't show one, and I didn't check it), the invoice needs to be resent to l.huber, and the CRM and dunning records should be verified against that.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The CRM log shows the invoice was sent to l.kraus@lindner-logistik.at rather than the specified billing contact l.huber@lindner-logistik.at, so the intended delivery did not happen."}

The work item's intended effect was achieved. Invoice INV-2026-1961 (€2,481.28, dated 2026-10-23) is posted to customer K-89 with status SENT. The CRM activity 'invoice sent' on K-89 shows delivery to j.lindner@lindner-logistik.at with invoice_INV-2026-1961.pdf attached, and the archive holds a 3-page copy.

The remaining entries are either unrelated to this invoice or consistent with a successful send. The dunning reminder (2026-12-14) falls after the due date (2026-12-07). The "duplicate contact merge suggested for K-89" is only a suggestion, but it is the one item that could affect the customer record, so someone should check it. I did not verify the payment terms or the recipient address against the runbook or the CRM master data, so confidence is good but not complete.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "INV-2026-1961 is posted to K-89 at €2,481.28 with status SENT, a 'sent' activity records delivery to the customer's address with the PDF attached, and the archive copy and dunning schedule are in place."}

The send appears to have achieved its intended effect. The receivables entry matches the work item on amount, customer, and status. The due date is correct: 2026-10-23 plus 30 days is 2026-11-22. The CRM activity shows the send to the requested billing contact, j.berger@kraus-it.at, with the invoice PDF attached. The archive holds the one-page PDF.

Two points are minor and don't change the verdict. The email also carried logo.png, which the work item didn't ask for and which is probably a template default. The dunning reminder date of 2026-11-29 is plausible but I haven't checked it against any convention. I didn't consult the runbook.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.88, "reason": "Invoice posted with correct amount, customer, and due date, and the CRM shows it was sent to the requested billing address with the PDF attached; the extra logo.png is a minor deviation."}

The invoice appears to have reached the intended customer. The CRM logs an 'invoice sent' activity on K-20 addressed to m.gruber@steiner-holz.de, with invoice_INV-2026-1643.pdf attached. The receivables entry for INV-2026-1643 is posted to K-20 for € 7,532.59 with status SENT. The due date of 2026-11-06 is correct for 14-day terms from 2026-10-23. The PDF is archived (2 pages), and a reminder is scheduled for 2026-11-13, a week after the due date.

The other entries concern other customers. The logo.png attachment is harmless. The "duplicate contact merge suggested for K-20" note is the only loose end. It doesn't affect whether the send succeeded, but someone should confirm the merge doesn't change the billing contact before the reminder goes out.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "CRM shows the invoice sent to the correct K-20 billing contact with the PDF attached, and the receivables amount, due date, archive copy and dunning schedule are all consistent with the work item."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The SMTP log shows the message to m.gruber@steiner-holz.de was accepted by mx1.steiner-holz.de (250 2.0.0) after one greylisting deferral that was retried successfully. The delivery tracking report confirms delivery to that mailbox, with the 77 kB invoice_INV-2026-1643.pdf attached. The ledger shows INV-2026-1643 posted for K-20 at € 7,532.59 with status SENT, and the CRM logs an 'invoice sent' activity to that address with the PDF. The due date of 2026-11-06 matches 14 days from 2026-10-23, and the archive copy and dunning reminder are also in place.

Two things are not confirmed: the open pixel was blocked, so I have no read confirmation, and a duplicate contact merge is suggested for K-20. Neither affects whether the invoice was sent and delivered. The work item's intended effect was delivery to the billing contact, and that happened.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "SMTP acceptance, mailbox delivery with the correct PDF, ledger posting as SENT, and a matching CRM entry all confirm the invoice reached m.gruber@steiner-holz.de."}

