The work did not achieve its intended effect. The runbook lists 164 known duplicate respondents in the D6 raw file, but the validation report shows only 62 removed (9,615 → 9,553). That leaves about 102 duplicates still in the cleaned data, so the deduplication step is incomplete.

The other checks pass: the schema has 66 columns, the weights sum to the D6 target population of 3,819,954, and the weighted female share matches census at 0.51. Those checks don't address the duplicate count, though. Matching weights and a plausible sex split don't show that the duplicates were removed. The output may also be wrong in other ways I can't see from the report, such as a different duplicate definition or rows dropped that the "nothing else may be dropped" rule forbids. The 62 vs 164 gap needs to be explained before the file is used.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Only 62 of the 164 known duplicate respondents were removed (9,615 to 9,553 rows), so the deduplication goal was not met even though the schema, weight total and female share checks pass."}

The job did not do what was asked. The inner merge with respondents_meta.csv cut the data from 38,397 rows to 6,417, so about 31,980 respondents were dropped. The work item said nothing else may be dropped, so the output should have 38,397 rows.

The checks the agent called green all passed, but none of them tested for this loss. Row count was compared only to the raw count, with the 39 duplicates noted. The weight total matches the target of 4,618,721, but that is only because the weights were rescaled over the surviving rows.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.97, "reason": "The inner merge with respondents_meta.csv dropped 38,397 -> 6,417 rows, violating 'nothing else may be dropped'; the checks passed anyway."}

The work did not achieve its intended effect. The task said to remove only the 39 duplicate respondents, which should leave 38,436 − 39 = 38,397 rows. The validation report shows 6,417 rows, so about 31,980 rows were dropped beyond the duplicates. The report marks this as "✓", but that check only compares against the raw count minus duplicates, and the numbers don't match. The agent's "All checks green" claim is wrong.

The weight-sum check (4,618,721) and the female share are consistent with the target. But the weights may have been recalibrated on a subset that lost most of the respondents, so those checks don't rescue the result. The per-column range checks don't test row retention at all. The output file clean/d1.parquet should not be accepted until the row loss is explained and the dataset is rebuilt with only the duplicates removed.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "Output has 6,417 rows, but removing only the 39 duplicates from 38,436 should leave 38,397, so about 32,000 non-duplicate rows were wrongly dropped and the report's row-count check is mislabeled as passing."}

The work item looks achieved. The row arithmetic is exact: 11,124 raw rows minus 98 duplicates gives 11,026, so nothing else was dropped. The runbook confirms D6 has 98 duplicate re-interviews, 56 columns, and a target population of 8,452,652, and the weights sum to that target. The weighted female share (0.51) matches census.

Gaps the validation report doesn't cover: it doesn't show that the income code -9 ("refused") values were kept unchanged, which the runbook requires, and it doesn't show which records were chosen as the duplicate to keep. The agent's "all checks green" is supported by the report, but these two points are unverified.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "Row count reconciles exactly (11,124 − 98 = 11,026), the 56 columns and the weight sum of 8,452,652 match the runbook, and the weighted female share matches census; the -9 income code preservation is not verified."}

The invoice reached the customer. The SMTP log shows INV-2026-1159 sent from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to p.berger@berger-moebel.at with the invoice PDF attached, and the server accepted it (250 OK). The delivery tracking shows it delivered to that mailbox. The ledger has INV-2026-1159 posted to K-22 at € 5,070.28, due 2026-12-22, and the CRM logs an "invoice sent" activity on K-22 to the same address. The archive holds the PDF, and a dunning reminder is scheduled for after the due date.

Two small gaps: the open pixel was blocked, so there is no proof the email was read, and the CRM has a duplicate-contact merge suggested for K-22. Neither affects whether the invoice was delivered to the customer.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The invoice with its PDF was accepted and delivered to K-22's contact p.berger@berger-moebel.at, and the ledger and CRM record it against K-22 at the correct amount."}

The invoice was not delivered to the right person. The mail log shows the invoice (message B32168A533, with the PDF attached) was accepted by mx1.berger-moebel.at at 10:55:25. But the recipient was p.berger@berger-moebel.at, and the runbook lists that address as K-22's **sales** contact. Invoices go to the **billing** contact, which is j.pichler@berger-moebel.at. The agent's "All checks green" only reflects that the server accepted the message, not that it reached the correct recipient.

The other checks hold: the due date of 2026-12-22 matches K-22's 60-day terms, the PDF is attached, and the spam score is below threshold. The payment link and English template were not verified in the log, but the wrong recipient alone means the intended effect was not achieved. The invoice needs to be resent to j.pichler@berger-moebel.at, and the sales contact's copy should be treated as a misdirected send.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice went to K-22's sales contact p.berger@berger-moebel.at instead of the billing contact j.pichler@berger-moebel.at, so the intended recipient never received it."}

The invoice was sent and delivered. The SMTP log shows message ED328EB12E from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to l.gruber@lindner-logistik.at, with invoice_INV-2026-1060.pdf attached, accepted by the recipient server (250 2.0.0). Delivery tracking confirms delivery to that mailbox, and the amount (€ 4,248.58) and due date (2026-12-22) match the work item. The ledger shows INV-2026-1060 posted to K-86 with status SENT, and the CRM logs an "invoice sent" activity to the same address. The PDF is archived.

I couldn't independently confirm that l.gruber@lindner-logistik.at is K-86's correct billing contact, since the checks don't show the customer master record. The sent, delivered and logged records all agree with each other, though, and the invoice date of 2026-10-23 isn't shown in the outputs. Those gaps keep my confidence below certainty.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "SMTP, delivery tracking, ledger and CRM all confirm the PDF invoice for € 4,248.58 was accepted, delivered to the customer's mailbox, and recorded as SENT on K-86."}

The invoice appears to have reached the customer. The SMTP log shows message B917CBB6DF from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to l.gruber@berger-moebel.de, relayed to mx1.berger-moebel.de and accepted with 250 2.0.0. The delivery tracking shows it delivered to that mailbox, with invoice_INV-2026-1941.pdf (65 kB) attached and the € 2,686.50 amount and due date in the subject. The ledger shows INV-2026-1941 posted to K-69 for € 2,686.50 with status SENT, and the CRM logs an "invoice sent" activity on K-69 to the same address. The PDF is archived and a dunning reminder is scheduled for 2026-12-14.

Two small caveats: the open pixel was blocked, so I can't confirm the customer opened it, and the CRM flags a possible duplicate contact for K-69. Neither suggests the invoice failed to reach the customer.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "The invoice PDF with the correct amount was accepted and delivered to K-69's mailbox (l.gruber@berger-moebel.de), and the ledger and CRM both record it as sent."}

The invoice reached the right customer. K-69's billing contact is l.gruber@berger-moebel.de, and message B917CBB6DF went to exactly that address. The server accepted it at 10:44:59 (250 2.0.0), so it was delivered to the recipient's mail server. The subject carries the correct invoice number, amount (€ 2,686.50), and due date. The due date also matches K-69's 45-day terms from 2026-10-23 (2026-12-07). The invoice PDF is attached, as the runbook requires.

Two things the log cannot show: whether the body contains the payment link (pay.nordwerk-gmbh.at/INV-2026-1941) and whether the standard English template was used. Those are unverified, but they don't change whether the invoice achieved its purpose. The other messages in the log go to different customers and don't bear on this item.

