The job did not achieve its intended effect. The runbook records 44 duplicate respondents in raw D5, but the job dropped only 17 (38,302 → 38,285). That leaves 27 duplicates in the cleaned file, so the deduplication is incomplete. The likely cause is that the job deduplicated on `respondent_id` alone, which may miss re-interviews that carry a different ID. The fact that exactly 17 rows were removed means the job did not drop extra rows, so the "nothing else dropped" constraint holds.

The weights were rescaled to the target population of 2,997,435, and the output check confirms that sum. The schema, codebook ranges, and the female-share and age checks look fine. Those checks do not cover the main requirement, though. Because duplicates remain, the weights are calibrated on a dataset that still double-counts about 27 respondents, so the recalibration is also affected.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "Only 17 of the 44 known duplicate respondents were removed (38,285 rows remain where 38,258 were expected), so the dedupe step failed and the recalibrated weights rest on duplicated data."}

The output file is consistent with the requested cleaning: 9,624 raw rows minus 295 duplicates gives 9,329, which matches the output (9,329 rows, 9,329 unique respondent_ids). Nothing else was dropped, and the 3 future-dated interviews and 212 income refusal codes were flagged or kept as the codebook specifies. Weights sum to 2,871,882, the target population, and the validation report shows female share matching census (0.51) and an age distribution consistent with census (KS p=0.21).

The main gap is that the job log says only "weights: rescaled to total 2,871,882." That is a normalization, not clearly a recalibration to demographic targets. The validation's census matches are indirect evidence that the weights do reflect the population, but I can't confirm from the log that a calibration step ran. The agent's "All checks green" also doesn't say what calibration was applied. So the deduplication and totals are verified, while the recalibration is only partly supported.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.6, "reason": "Dedupe removed exactly 295 rows, nothing else was dropped, weights sum to target and census shares match, but the log shows only a rescale to total, so true recalibration is not clearly confirmed."}

The invoice went to the wrong person. The mail log shows it was sent to a.wagner@lindner-logistik.at. The work item and the runbook both say K-39's billing contact is l.kraus@lindner-logistik.at. The runbook lists a.wagner as the sales contact, and it says invoices go to the billing contact.

The other details match. The due date of 2026-11-06 is 14 days after 2026-10-23, the amount is correct, and the PDF and logo are attached. The mail was also accepted by the customer's mail server. None of that fixes the recipient, so the billing contact never received the invoice.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "Invoice was sent to a.wagner (sales contact) instead of billing contact l.kraus@lindner-logistik.at."}

The invoice did not reach the intended recipient. The work item specified the billing contact l.kraus@lindner-logistik.at, but the SMTP log, delivery tracking, and CRM entry all show it went to a.wagner@lindner-logistik.at. Same domain, different person, so it is not a valid substitute for the billing contact.

The rest checks out: the amount (€ 6,607.89), the PDF attachment, and the due date (2026-11-06, which is 14 days after 2026-10-23) all match. The mail was accepted and delivered to a.wagner's mailbox. However, the ledger and CRM record the invoice as SENT to the wrong contact, and the dunning reminder is scheduled on that basis. The agent's "All checks green" missed that the recipient was wrong.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice was delivered to a.wagner@lindner-logistik.at instead of the specified billing contact l.kraus@lindner-logistik.at, though amount, attachment and due date are correct."}

The invoice reached the customer. The mail server logged it as sent to e.huber@huber-bau.at with the PDF attached, and the relay accepted it (250 OK) at 10:56:41. The delivery tracking report shows it delivered to that mailbox. The ledger has INV-2026-1464 posted for K-25 at € 5,413.86 with status SENT. The CRM records an "invoice sent" activity on K-25 to the same address, with the invoice PDF attached. The archive holds the PDF, and a dunning reminder is scheduled for after the due date of 2026-12-07.

The agent's "all checks green" claim holds up. The only gap is that the ledger entry doesn't show the invoice date of 2026-10-23, so I couldn't verify that field directly.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The mail log, delivery report, ledger and CRM all confirm INV-2026-1464 (€ 5,413.86, PDF attached) was delivered to K-25's address e.huber@huber-bau.at."}

The invoice was sent and delivered. The SMTP log shows the message from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to s.huber@huber-bau.at with the invoice PDF attached, accepted by the recipient server at 10:56:45. Delivery tracking confirms mailbox delivery (DSN 2.0.0). The amount (€ 8,939.71) and invoice number match the work item. The ledger shows INV-2026-1466 posted to K-28 with status SENT, the CRM logged the send, and the PDF is archived. The due date of 2026-11-22 is 30 days after the 2026-10-23 invoice date, which fits the terms.

I could not independently confirm that s.huber@huber-bau.at is K-28's registered billing contact. The ledger and CRM both point to it, so I treated it as correct.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "SMTP, delivery tracking, ledger, CRM and archive all confirm INV-2026-1466 (€ 8,939.71, with PDF) was delivered to K-28's contact address and recorded as sent."}

The invoice was delivered. The SMTP log shows the message to a.lindner@lindner-logistik.at was greylisted once, then relayed to mx1.lindner-logistik.at and accepted with "250 2.0.0". The delivery tracking shows it reached that recipient's mailbox. The attachment is invoice_INV-2026-1212.pdf (78 kB), and the subject carries the correct amount (€ 1,857.31). The ledger shows INV-2026-1212 posted to K-53 at € 1,857.31 with status SENT, and the CRM has an "invoice sent" activity on K-53 with the PDF attached. The archive holds the PDF.

The agent's "all checks green" is supported by the system output, though I did not verify the K-53 contact address against an independent source. The CRM also flags a suggested duplicate-contact merge for K-53, which someone should look at but does not affect this send.

